  "description": "Project pooled [CLS] token representation through binary linear classification head to evaluate IsNext sentence probability.\n\nBERT utilizes Next Sentence Prediction alongside Masked Language Modeling to learn inter-sentence discourse relationships and semantic coherence.\n\n### Mathematical Formulation\n$$\n\\mathbf{z} = \\mathbf{h}_{\\text{[CLS]}} \\mathbf{W} + \\mathbf{b}, \\quad P(\\text{IsNext}) = \\text{Softmax}(\\mathbf{z})_0\n$$\n\n### Step-by-Step Implementation\n1. Project CLS vector through projection weights W and bias b\n2. Compute softmax probability distribution over classes [IsNext, NotNext]\n3. Assign binary boolean label is_next and return dictionary rounded to 4 decimal places\n\n### Returns\nDictionary with 'logits', 'probabilities', and boolean 'is_next'.",
  "theory": "## Understanding the Theory: Next Sentence Prediction\n\n### Core Intuition: Why Do We Need This?\nNatural language consists of discrete words and symbols arranged in sequential order. To process text with neural networks, we convert words into continuous embedding vectors and model their grammatical and semantic relationships. **Next Sentence Prediction** is a core sequence-processing mechanism used in modern Transformer and LLM architectures.\n\nBERT utilizes Next Sentence Prediction alongside Masked Language Modeling to learn inter-sentence discourse relationships and semantic coherence. In this problem, the goal is to: project pooled [cls] token representation through binary linear classification head to evaluate isnext sentence probability.\n\n### Mathematical Principles & Formulation\nThe core governing equation for this operation is:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{z} = \\mathbf{h}_{\\text{[CLS]}} \\mathbf{W} + \\mathbf{b}, \\quad P(\\text{IsNext}) = \\text{Softmax}(\\mathbf{z})_0\n$$\n\nEvery term in this formulation plays a precise mathematical role in ensuring dimensional consistency, numerical stability, and correct algorithmic behavior.\n\n### Step-by-Step Computational Workflow\n1. **Phase 1**: Project CLS vector through projection weights W and bias b\n2. **Phase 2**: Compute softmax probability distribution over classes [IsNext, NotNext]\n3. **Phase 3**: Assign binary boolean label is_next and return dictionary rounded to 4 decimal places\n\n### Practical Machine Learning Applications & Nuances\n* **Self-Attention Mechanics**: Scaled dot-product attention computes all-to-all token interactions dynamically, allowing tokens to gather context from the entire sentence simultaneously.\n* **Position Encodings**: Because self-attention is permutation-invariant, positional encodings (like sinusoidal or Rotary Position Embeddings) inject word order into the representations.\n* **Vocabulary & Subwords**: Algorithms like BPE tokenize text into frequent subwords, allowing models to represent rare words without requiring an infinite vocabulary.",
  "examples": [
    "Input: cls_representation = [1.0, -0.5], W = [[2.0, -1.0], [0.5, 1.0]], b = [0.1, -0.1]\n\nOutput: {\"logits\": [1.85, -1.6], \"probabilities\": [0.9692, 0.0308], \"is_next\": 1}\n\nExplanation: Evaluated nlp_next_sentence_prediction under standard test conditions."
